'''Amy Jean Klobuchar''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|l|oʊ|b|ə|ʃ|ɑr}} {{Respell|KLOH|bə|shar}}; born May 25, 1960) is an American politician and lawyer serving as the [[Seniority in the United States Senate|senior]] [[United States Senate|United States senator]] from [[Minnesota]], a seat she has held since 2007. A member of the [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party]] (DFL), Minnesota's affiliate of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she served from 1999 to 2007 as [[District attorney|county attorney]] of [[Hennepin County, Minnesota]]. She is the Democratic nominee in the [[2026 Minnesota gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{Cite web| title= US Senator Amy Klobuchar announces bid for Minnesota governor| url= https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cq8413k1xvno| website= www.bbc.com| date= 2026-01-29| access-date= 2026-01-31| language= en-GB}}</ref>

Born in [[Plymouth, Minnesota]], Klobuchar graduated from [[Yale University]] and the [[University of Chicago Law School]]. She was a partner at two [[Minneapolis]] law firms before being elected [[County Attorney|county attorney]] of [[Hennepin County]] in 1998, making her responsible for all [[criminal prosecution]] in Minnesota's most populous county. Klobuchar was first elected to the Senate in [[2006 United States Senate election in Minnesota|2006]], succeeding [[Mark Dayton]] to become Minnesota's first elected [[Women in the United States Senate|female United States senator]]. She became Minnesota's senior senator in 2009, when [[Norm Coleman]] left the Senate following his [[2008 United States Senate election in Minnesota|defeat]]. She was reelected by a landslide in [[2012 United States Senate election in Minnesota|2012]], winning 85 of the state's 87 counties, before being reelected again in [[2018 United States Senate election in Minnesota|2018]].<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://www.businessinsider.com/amy-klobuchar-2020-democrat-president-elected-minnesota-reactions-2018-11| title= Amy Klobuchar is being talked up as a 2020 contender who could 'bury' Trump after she won re-election in Minnesota |author= Bostock, Bill| date= November 7, 2018| work= Business Insider| access-date= November 9, 2018| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181109153358/https://www.businessinsider.com/amy-klobuchar-2020-democrat-president-elected-minnesota-reactions-2018-11| archive-date= November 9, 2018| url-status=live}}</ref> Klobuchar's political positions align with [[Modern liberalism in the United States|modern liberalism]]. She has focused on healthcare reform, [[consumer protection]], [[Abortion-rights movements|abortion rights]], [[agriculture]], and [[climate change]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stolberg |first=Sheryl Gay |date=November 26, 2018 |title=Amy Klobuchar Is 'Minnesota Nice.' But Is That What Democrats Want for 2020? |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/26/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-2020-election.html |access-date= December 22, 2022 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=December 22, 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221222053555/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/11/26/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-2020-election.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=DeLong |first1=Matt |last2=Tribune |first2=Chase Davis |title= How Amy Klobuchar fared in Trump country |url= https://www.startribune.com/how-amy-klobuchar-fared-in-trump-country/498035261/ |access-date= December 22, 2022 |website= Star Tribune |date= November 25, 2018 |language=en |archive-date= December 22, 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221222053550/https://www.startribune.com/how-amy-klobuchar-fared-in-trump-country/498035261/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Halper |first1=Evan |date=February 10, 2019 |title= Sen. Amy Klobuchar offers Democrats a Midwestern road to the White House |url= https://www.latimes.com/politics/la-na-pol-amy-klobuchar-president-election-20190210-story.html |access-date= April 16, 2023 |website= Los Angeles Times |language=en-US |archive-date=April 23, 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230423203900/https://www.latimes.com/politics/la-na-pol-amy-klobuchar-president-election-20190210-story.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

On February 10, 2019, Klobuchar announced [[Amy Klobuchar 2020 presidential campaign|her candidacy]] for the [[2020 Democratic Party presidential primaries|Democratic nomination]] in the [[2020 United States presidential election]]; on March 2, 2020, she suspended her campaign and endorsed [[Joe Biden]].<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://www.politico.com/news/2020/03/02/klobuchar-to-drop-out-of-2020-campaign-endorse-biden-118823| title= Klobuchar to drop out of 2020 campaign, endorse Biden| last= Schneider| first= Elena| date= March 2, 2020| work= Politico| access-date= March 2, 2020| language= en| archive-date= June 18, 2020| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200618074839/https://www.politico.com/news/2020/03/02/klobuchar-to-drop-out-of-2020-campaign-endorse-biden-118823| url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web| url= https://www.cnn.com/2020/03/02/politics/amy-klobuchar-ends-2020-campaign/index.html| title= Amy Klobuchar ends 2020 presidential campaign and endorses Joe Biden| author1= Dan Merica| author2= Kyung Lah| author3= Jasmine Wright |author4= Kate Sullivan| website= CNN| date= March 2, 2020| access-date= March 3, 2020| archive-date= March 2, 2020| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200302195014/https://www.cnn.com/2020/03/02/politics/amy-klobuchar-ends-2020-campaign/index.html| url-status= live}}</ref> In 2021, she became the chair of the [[Senate Rules Committee]]. She was reelected to a fourth Senate term in [[2024 United States Senate election in Minnesota|2024]], defeating Republican nominee [[Royce White]]. In January 2026, she announced her candidacy for governor of Minnesota after incumbent [[Tim Walz]] withdrew from the race.<ref name= "gov1">{{cite web |last1= Lerer |first1= Lisa |last2= Epstein |first2= Reid J. |last3= Londoño |first3= Ernesto |title= Amy Klobuchar Files Papers for Run to Succeed Tim Walz as Minnesota Governor |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/22/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-minnesota-governor-tim-walz.html |publisher= The New York Times |access-date= 23 January 2026}}</ref><ref name= "gov2">{{cite web |last1= Barrow |first1= Bill |last2= Karnowski |first2= Steve |date= January 29, 2026 |title= Democratic Sen. Klobuchar says she's running for Minnesota governor after Gov. Walz dropped out |url= https://apnews.com/article/klobuchar-minnesota-governor-walz-trump-deec28156a6ed2aca6b12971824d6e3e |publisher= [[APNews.com]] |access-date= January 29, 2026}}</ref>
{{TOC limit|3}}

==Early life and education==
Klobuchar was born in [[Plymouth, Minnesota]], the daughter of Rose (née Heuberger) and [[Jim Klobuchar]]. Her mother taught second grade until she retired at age 70.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Nelson|first=Tim|url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2010/08/02/rose-klobuchar|title=Rose Klobuchar, mother of Sen. Amy Klobuchar, dies|access-date=February 22, 2017|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170223125452/https://www.mprnews.org/story/2010/08/02/rose-klobuchar|website=MPR News|date=August 2, 2010|archive-date=February 23, 2017}}</ref> Her father was a sportswriter and columnist for the ''[[Star Tribune]]''.<ref>{{Cite news | first=Doug|last=Smith|url=http://www.startribune.com/born-to-ride-jim-klobuchar-and-the-birth-of-the-minnesota-bike-tour/320920321/ | title=Born to ride: Jim Klobuchar and the birth of the Minnesota bike tour | newspaper=[[Star Tribune]] | date=August 7, 2015| access-date=February 22, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170223044030/http://www.startribune.com/born-to-ride-jim-klobuchar-and-the-birth-of-the-minnesota-bike-tour/320920321/ | archive-date=February 23, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> He was of [[Slovene American|Slovene]] descent and her mother of [[German-speaking Switzerland|German-Swiss]] ancestry.<ref name=svet/><ref name=delo/>

Klobuchar's parents divorced when she was 15. The divorce took a toll on the family; her relationship with her father was not fully restored until he quit drinking in the 1990s.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RkhsCgAAQBAJ|title=The Senator Next Door: A Memoir from the Heartland|last=Klobuchar|first=Amy|date=2015|publisher=[[Macmillan Publishers|Macmillan]]|location=New York City|isbn=978-1627794176|language=en}}</ref>

She attended public schools in Plymouth and was [[valedictorian]] at [[Wayzata High School]],<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.wayzata.k12.mn.us/Page/7803 | title=WHS Involvement / WHS Distinguished Alumni | website=wayzata.k12.mn.us | language=en | access-date=March 16, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170317054642/http://www.wayzata.k12.mn.us/Page/7803 | archive-date=March 17, 2017 | url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.amyklobuchar.com/about | title=About Amy | website=Amy Klobuchar for U.S. Senate | language=en | access-date=March 16, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170425061726/http://www.amyklobuchar.com/about | archive-date=April 25, 2017 | url-status=dead | df=mdy-all }}</ref> where she was also class treasurer and secretary.<ref>Dzhanova, Yelena, and Hurwitz, Kelsey, "Most Likely to Be President," ''New York'', April 1–14, 2019, p. 12</ref> She received her [[Bachelor of Arts]] ''[[Latin honors|magna cum laude]]'' in [[political science]] in 1982 from [[Yale University]].<ref>1982 ''Yale Banner,'' p. 394.</ref> While at Yale, Klobuchar spent time as an intern for former vice president and former Minnesota senator [[Walter Mondale]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite magazine |url=http://www.elle.com/culture/career-politics/a11015/the-audacity-of-minnesota-senator-amy-klobuchar-439048/ | title=The Audacity of Minnesota: Meet Senator Amy Klobuchar | last=DePaulo | first=Lisa | magazine=[[Elle (magazine)|ELLE]] | date=March 30, 2010| access-date=May 14, 2017 | language=en | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170503105813/http://www.elle.com/culture/career-politics/a11015/the-audacity-of-minnesota-senator-amy-klobuchar-439048/ | archive-date=May 3, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> Her senior thesis, ''Uncovering the Dome'',<ref>{{cite book | last = Klobuchar | first = Amy | title = Uncovering the Dome | year=1986 | publisher = Waveland Press | edition = reprint | isbn = 978-0-88133-218-6}}</ref> a 250-page history of the ten years of politics surrounding the building of the [[Hubert H. Humphrey Metrodome]] in Minneapolis, was published by Waveland Press in 1986. After Yale, Klobuchar enrolled at the [[University of Chicago Law School]], where she served as an associate editor of the ''[[University of Chicago Law Review]]'' and earned her [[Juris Doctor|J.D.]] with high honors in 1985.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/politics/elections/ct-cb-amy-klobuchar-2020-election-bio-20191215-vt7kt33uz5fgzktxdgrfx7ciwu-story.html|title=Who is Amy Klobuchar?|date=December 14, 2019|newspaper=[[Chicago Tribune]]|publisher=[[Tribune Media Services]]|location=Chicago, IL|access-date=January 7, 2020|archive-date=October 19, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231019011432/https://www.chicagotribune.com/politics/elections/ct-cb-amy-klobuchar-2020-election-bio-20191215-vt7kt33uz5fgzktxdgrfx7ciwu-story.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Early career==
After law school, Klobuchar worked as a [[corporate lawyer]].<ref name= ":0" /> In time, she became a partner at the Minnesota law firms [[Dorsey & Whitney]] and [[Gray Plant Mooty]], where she specialized in "regulatory work in telecommunications law".<ref>{{cite web |url= http://bioguide.congress.gov/scripts/biodisplay.pl?index=k000367 | title= Klobuchar, Amy – Biographical Information | website= bioguide.congress.gov| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20070201220009/http://bioguide.congress.gov/scripts/biodisplay.pl?index=K000367| archive-date= February 1, 2007| url-status= dead| access-date= May 14, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url= http://minnesota.publicradio.org/collections/special/2006/campaign/senate/klobuchar/ | title= MPR: Campaign 2006: U.S. Senate: Amy Klobuchar| website= Minnesota Public Radio|  language= en |access-date= May 14, 2017 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170322122031/http://minnesota.publicradio.org/collections/special/2006/campaign/senate/klobuchar/ |archive-date= March 22, 2017 | url-status= live |df= mdy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.mprnews.org/story/2019/02/10/amy-klobuchar-timeline| title= Klobuchar timeline: A life of law and politics led to presidential aspirations| first1= Briana| last1= Bierschbach| first2= Cody| last2= Nelson| first3= Jiwon| last3= Choi| work= MPR News| date= February 10, 2019| access-date= September 18, 2020| archive-date= October 17, 2023| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20231017175959/https://www.mprnews.org/story/2019/02/10/amy-klobuchar-timeline| url-status= live}}</ref> Her first foray into the political sphere came after she gave birth and was forced to leave the hospital 24 hours later, a situation exacerbated because her daughter, Abigail,<ref name= ":0" /> was born with a disorder that prevented her from swallowing. The experience led Klobuchar to appear before the [[Minnesota Legislature]],{{when| date= August 2026}} advocating for a bill guaranteeing new mothers a 48-hour hospital stay. Minnesota passed the bill and President [[Bill Clinton]] later made the policy federal law.<ref name= ":0" />

Klobuchar was subsequently active in supporting [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party|Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor]] (DFL) candidates, including [[Hennepin County, Minnesota|Hennepin County]] Attorney [[Michael O. Freeman]]. She first sought public office in 1994, when Freeman ran for Minnesota governor, pledging to drop out of the nonpartisan Hennepin County Attorney race if Freeman chose instead to seek reelection to that position. When Freeman did not win the DFL endorsement for governor, Klobuchar stepped aside in June 1994 and supported Freeman for reelection.<ref>{{cite book| url= https://books.google.com/books?id=WuTCBwAAQBAJ&q=mike+freeman+amy+klobuchar+1994&pg=PA115 |title= The Senator Next Door: A Memoir from the Heartland |author= Amy Klobuchar |via= Books.google.com |year= 2015 |publisher= Henry Holt and Company |access-date= December 14, 2018| isbn= 978-1627794183 }}</ref>

==Hennepin County attorney (1999–2007)==
Klobuchar was elected [[List of county attorneys of Hennepin County|Hennepin County attorney]] in 1998, running after Freeman declined to seek an additional term.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.msnbc.com/transcripts/rachel-maddow-show/2015-09-01 | title=The Rachel Maddow Show| date=September 1, 2015 | work=MSNBC | access-date=May 14, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180115001249/http://www.msnbc.com/transcripts/rachel-maddow-show/2015-09-01 | archive-date=January 15, 2018 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref><ref name=":0" />  In the nonpartisan election, she defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] Sheryl Ramstad Hvass by a margin of less than 1%. Klobuchar was reelected unopposed in 2002.<ref name="MPRtimeline1">{{cite web |last1=Bierschbach |first1=Briana |last2=Nelson |first2=Cody |last3=Choi |first3=Jiwon |title=Timeline: A look back at Klobuchar's years |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2019/02/10/amy-klobuchar-timeline |website=MPR News |access-date=8 August 2023 |language=en |date=10 February 2019 |archive-date=October 17, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017175959/https://www.mprnews.org/story/2019/02/10/amy-klobuchar-timeline |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2001, ''Minnesota Lawyer'' named her "Attorney of the Year".<ref>"Amy Klobuchar." ''Gale Biography Online Collection''. Gale, 2007. Retrieved via ''Gale In Context: Biography'' database, September 17, 2019.</ref><ref name="Minnesota Lawyer">{{cite news |url=http://minnlawyer.com/2001/12/31/minnesota-lawyer-recognizes-10-as-8216attorneys-of-the-year8217-2/ | title=Minnesota Lawyer recognizes 10 as 'Attorneys of the Year' | work=Minnesota Lawyer | date=December 31, 2001 | access-date=March 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170322111747/http://minnlawyer.com/2001/12/31/minnesota-lawyer-recognizes-10-as-8216attorneys-of-the-year8217-2/ | archive-date=March 22, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}{{subscription required}}</ref> Klobuchar was president of the Minnesota County Attorneys Association from November 2002 to November 2003.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=01nm_wrOOXcC&q=Klobuchar%20Minnesota%20County%20Attorneys%20Association&pg=PT82 | title=51 Women Senators?: Will We Ever Have 51 Women Senators? When? How Will They Represent Us? | last1=Herzig | first1=Frolik & Winnie Frolik & Billy|last2=Herzig|first2=Winnie Frolik & Billy | year=2017 | publisher=iUniverse | isbn=978-1440193033 | language=en}}</ref>

In 2002, Klobuchar spearheaded an effort that resulted in state laws being altered to allow felony charges to be brought against repeat [[drunk driving]] offenders. In 2003, Klobuchar dealt with one of her highest-profile cases when the Hennepin County Attorney's Office brought several charges against former professional baseball player [[Kirby Puckett]] related to an alleged sex crime. Puckett was acquitted by a jury of all charges brought against him.<ref name="MPRtimeline1"/>

Klobuchar took a "tough-on-crime" approach. At the time she took office, there was a crime wave in Minneapolis, with the city's murder rate much higher than the national rate. Klobuchar had won election on the slogan "More Trials, More Convictions". She pursued heavier sentencing and less favorable [[plea deal]]s, and sought to bring more cases to trial. By the end of her first year, she had significantly increased the number of cases brought and convictions secured.<ref name="OpelFausset1">{{cite web |last1=Opel |first1=Richard A. Jr. |last2=Fausset |first2=Richard |title=Klobuchar ramped up prosecutions, except in cases against police |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/02/26/us/klobuchar-prosecutor-myon-burrell.html |website=The New York Times |access-date=8 August 2023 |date=26 February 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225106/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/02/26/us/klobuchar-prosecutor-myon-burrell.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Under Klobuchar, the Hennepin County Attorney's Office prosecuted people for possession of [[khat]]. At the time, it was rare in the United States for prosecutors to pursue prosecution on such charges.<ref name="Intercept1">{{cite web |last1=Lacy |first1=Akela |title=As a Prosecutor, Amy Klobuchar Went After Somali Users of Khat, an Herbal Stimulant |url=https://theintercept.com/2020/02/03/amy-klobuchar-drug-prosecutions-history/ |website=The Intercept |access-date=8 August 2023 |date=3 February 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225237/https://theintercept.com/2020/02/03/amy-klobuchar-drug-prosecutions-history/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Klobuchar also brought felony charges against a number of men for failure to pay [[child support]].<ref name="Lily1"/>

Klobuchar pursued tougher sentencing in hopes of deterring crime. For some smaller offenses, such as vandalism, she regularly sought sentences that exceeded the recommended duration. This was true for both adult and minor defendants. For property crime offenders who had five prior convictions, Klobuchar's office similarly pursued longer sentences than had been recommended.<ref name="Lily1">{{cite news |title=As a prosecutor in heavily white Minnesota, Amy Klobuchar didn't charge police involved in fatal encounters with black men |newspaper=Washington Post |url=https://www.thelily.com/as-a-prosecutor-in-heavily-white-minnesota-amy-klobuchar-didnt-charge-police-involved-in-fatal-encounters-with-black-men/ |access-date=8 August 2023 |date=March 27, 2019 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225135/https://www.thelily.com/as-a-prosecutor-in-heavily-white-minnesota-amy-klobuchar-didnt-charge-police-involved-in-fatal-encounters-with-black-men/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Klobuchar declared that she intended to scrutinize judges who were "letting offenders off the hook too easily". In 2000, a successful appeal by Klobuchar lengthened by two days a sentence that a judge gave an immigrant defendant, which placed the defendant at a greatly increased risk of deportation. The judge had originally imposed a sentence  of 364 days. A sentence of 365 days or greater was likely to lead to the defendant's deportation, and the two days Klobuchar added placed the defendant over that threshold. An analysis by the [[Vera Institute of Justice]] found that during Klobuchar's tenure, there was a 20% increase in the number of prison inmates in Hennepin County, which the analysis attributed to the harsher sentences Klobuchar sought.<ref name="OpelFausset1"/>

According to the Vera Institute of Justice, during Klobuchar's tenure there was a decrease in the disparity between the rates of [[African American]] and white rates of imprisonment, but the disparity remained pronounced<ref name="OpelFausset1"/> at quadruple the national average.<ref name="Lily1"/>

Klobuchar successfully prosecuted teenage defendant Myon Burrell for the 2002 gunshot murder of 11-year-old Tyesha Edwards. Both Burrell and Edwards were African American. For much of her subsequent career, Klobuchar highlighted this case, framing it both as an example of her toughness on crime as well as an example of her fighting to provide justice for African-American communities affected by gun crime. Burrell remained steadfast in claiming that he was innocent. In late January 2020, during Klobuchar's presidential campaign, the ''[[Associated Press]]'' called attention to flaws in the case against Burrell, and quoted a co-defendant in the trial as having admitted that he, not Burrell, had actually fired the shot that killed Edwards.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Stevens |first1=Matt |title=Klobuchar Faces Uproar Over Her Role in Black Teenager's Murder Conviction |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/01/31/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-myon-burrell.html |website=The New York Times |access-date=8 August 2023 |date=31 January 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225518/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/01/31/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-myon-burrell.html |url-status=live }}</ref> ''[[APM Reports]]'' also uncovered further consequential flaws with the case against Burrell.<ref>{{cite web |title=Minnesota juvenile lifer walks free after 18 years in prison |url=https://apnews.com/article/myon-burrell-case-commutes-life-sentence-97073acc5950020bb0f5791f73aa4aa4 |website=AP News |access-date=8 August 2023 |language=en |date=15 December 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225217/https://apnews.com/article/myon-burrell-case-commutes-life-sentence-97073acc5950020bb0f5791f73aa4aa4 |url-status=live }}</ref> Due to these circumstances, his sentence was [[Commutation (law)|commuted]] by the [[Minnesota Board of Pardons]] in December 2020.<ref>{{cite web |last1=McDowell |first1=Robin |last2=Mason |first2=Maggie |title='Clemency and mercy': Minnesota to free Myon Burrell, convicted in 2002 death of 11-year-old girl |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2020/12/15/myon-burrell-minnesota-free-man-2002-death/3914850001/ |website=USA Today |access-date=8 August 2023 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225217/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2020/12/15/myon-burrell-minnesota-free-man-2002-death/3914850001/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In [[2004 United States presidential election|2004]], Klobuchar supported [[John Kerry 2004 presidential campaign|the presidential campaign]] of U.S. senator [[John Kerry]], traveling across Minnesota as a campaign surrogate.<ref name="MPRtimeline1"/>

Klobuchar did not prosecute any cases related to [[police brutality]].<ref name="APM1"/> During her tenure, Hennepin County saw 30 recorded police-involved deaths.<ref>{{cite web |title=Police-involved deaths during tenure of Hennepin County Attorney Amy Klobuchar |url=https://m.startribune.com/police-involved-deaths-during-tenure-of-hennepin-county-attorney-amy-klobuchar/568259112/ |website=Star Tribune |access-date=8 August 2023 |language=en |date=February 27, 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225236/https://m.startribune.com/police-involved-deaths-during-tenure-of-hennepin-county-attorney-amy-klobuchar/568259112/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Some of these deaths resulted in public controversy, but Klobuchar did not bring any charges.<ref name="APM1">{{cite web |last1=Scheck |first1=Tom |last2=Williams |first2=Brandt |last3=Pederson |first3=Nikki |title=Klobuchar didn't prosecute controversial police killings or brutality cases as a county attorney |url=https://www.apmreports.org/story/2019/03/25/amy-klobuchar-police-hennepin-county-prosecutor |website=www.apmreports.org |publisher=American Public Media |access-date=8 August 2023 |language=en |date=March 25, 2019 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225311/https://www.apmreports.org/story/2019/03/25/amy-klobuchar-police-hennepin-county-prosecutor |url-status=live }}</ref> As was common practice at the time, especially in Minnesota, to determine whether to bring charges in such instances, Klobuchar relied on [[grand jury]] recommendations.<ref name="YearsAs1">{{cite web |last1=Montemayor |first1=Stephen |title=Amy Klobuchar's years as prosecutor get a hard new look with her 2020 run |url=https://m.startribune.com/amy-klobuchar-years-as-prosecutor-get-a-hard-new-look/568253532/ |website=Star Tribune |access-date=8 August 2023 |language=en |date=February 27, 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225159/https://m.startribune.com/amy-klobuchar-years-as-prosecutor-get-a-hard-new-look/568253532/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Critics of her use of grand juries alleged that it lacked transparency and was perhaps a tactic for Klobuchar to distance herself from responsibility for the decisions made.<ref name="OpelFausset1"/><ref name="YearsAs1"/> After the 2020 [[murder of George Floyd]] in Minneapolis Police custody, renewed attention was brought to Klobuchar's tenure as Hennepin County Attorney. She was retrospectively criticized for not prosecuting police misconduct during her tenure. Attention was also brought to the fact that during Klobuchar's tenure an officer-involved killing had occurred that involved Officer [[Derek Chauvin]], who later murdered Floyd. This killing did not result in prosecution. The killing occurred in October 2006, several months before Klobuchar left office, and a grand jury was empaneled by her successor (Freeman, who returned for a second stint as county attorney). The grand jury did not recommend any charges.<ref>Multiple sources:
*{{Cite web|title=Amy Klobuchar didn't prosecute officer at center of George Floyd's death after previous conduct complaints|url=https://www.theweek.com/speedreads/916926/amy-klobuchar-didnt-prosecute-officer-center-george-floyds-death-after-previous-conduct-complaints|date=May 28, 2020|work=The Week|language=en|access-date=May 28, 2020|archive-date=May 29, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200529133400/https://theweek.com/speedreads/916926/amy-klobuchar-didnt-prosecute-officer-center-george-floyds-death-after-previous-conduct-complaints|url-status=dead}}
*{{Cite web|title=Amy Klobuchar Criticized for Declining to Prosecute Police Shootings as Minnesota County Attorney|author=Mahadevan, Tara C.|url=https://www.complex.com/life/2020/05/amy-klobuchar-declined-prosecute-police-shootings|website=Complex|date=May 28, 2020 |language=en|access-date=May 28, 2020|archive-date=February 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210201131111/https://www.complex.com/life/2020/05/amy-klobuchar-declined-prosecute-police-shootings|url-status=live}}
*{{cite web |last1=King |first1=Ledyard |last2=Hayes |first2=Christal |title=Amy Klobuchar under scrutiny for 2006 case involving officer charged in George Floyd death |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/05/29/george-floyd-klobuchar-officer-charged/5282580002/ |website=USA Today |access-date=8 August 2023 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810225247/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/05/29/george-floyd-klobuchar-officer-charged/5282580002/ |url-status=live }}</ref> This renewed scrutiny arose several months after Klobuchar concluded her presidential campaign, and while reporting had named her as a potential [[running mate]] of [[Presumptive nominee|presumptive]] Democratic nominee [[Joe Biden]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Cillizza |first1=Chris |title=How Amy Klobuchar is trying to save her rapidly dwindling VP chances |url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/06/04/politics/amy-klobuchar-derek-chauvin-charges/index.html |website=CNN |access-date=8 August 2023 |language=en |date=4 June 2020 |archive-date=August 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230810232246/https://www.cnn.com/2020/06/04/politics/amy-klobuchar-derek-chauvin-charges/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==U.S. Senate (2007–present)==

===Elections===

==== 2006 ====
[[File:Senator Amy Klobuchar 2006.JPG|thumb|Official portrait, 2006.]]
{{main|2006 United States Senate election in Minnesota}}

In early 2005, after U.S. senator [[Mark Dayton]] announced that he would not seek reelection in 2006, Klobuchar became an early favorite for the [[Minnesota Democratic-Farmer-Labor Party|DFL]] nomination. She had originally planned to run for [[attorney general of Minnesota]], but was persuaded to run for Senate.<ref name="MPRtimeline1"/><ref name="WAPOblog1"/> [[EMILY's List|Emily's List]] endorsed her on September 29, 2005, and Klobuchar won the DFL endorsement on June 9, 2006. She gained the support of the majority of DFL state legislators in [[Minnesota]] during the primaries. A poll of DFL state delegates showed Klobuchar beating her then closest opponent, [[Patty Wetterling]], 66% to 15%. In January Wetterling dropped out of the race and endorsed Klobuchar. Former Senate candidate and prominent lawyer [[Mike Ciresi]], who was widely seen as a serious potential DFL candidate, indicated in early February that he would not enter the race; that was viewed as an important boost for Klobuchar.<ref name="WAPOblog1">[http://blog.washingtonpost.com/thefix/2006/02/the_friday_senate_line.html The Fix&nbsp;– The Friday Line: Can Democrats Get to 6?] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060224200139/http://blog.washingtonpost.com/thefix/2006/02/the_friday_senate_line.html | date=February 24, 2006 }}. Retrieved October 2, 2006.</ref>

In the general election Klobuchar faced [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] candidate [[Mark Kennedy (politician)|Mark Kennedy]], [[Independence Party of Minnesota|Independence Party]] candidate Robert Fitzgerald, [[Constitution Party of Minnesota|Constitution]] candidate Ben Powers, and [[Green Party (United States)|Green Party]] candidate Michael Cavlan. Klobuchar led in the polls throughout the campaign, and won with 58% of the vote to Kennedy's 38% and Fitzgerald's 3%, carrying 79 of Minnesota's 87 counties. She is the first woman to be elected U.S. senator from Minnesota. ([[Muriel Humphrey]], the state's first female senator and former [[second lady of the United States]], was appointed to fill [[Hubert Humphrey|her husband]]'s unexpired term and not elected.)<ref>{{Cite news|last=Mooar|first=Brian|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/politics/campaigns/junkie/links/humphrey.htm|title=Hubert Humphrey's Widow Dies at 86|date=September 21, 1998|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=February 12, 2020|archive-date=March 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309064714/https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/politics/campaigns/junkie/links/humphrey.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

==== 2012 ====
[[File:Amy Klobuchar campaign IMG 1288 tower mn.JPG|thumb|260px|Klobuchar's father, [[Jim Klobuchar|Jim]], and supporters campaigning for Klobuchar as U.S. senator, [[Tower, Minnesota]], July 4, 2012]]
{{main|2012 United States Senate election in Minnesota}}

Klobuchar won a second term in the U.S. Senate, defeating Republican state representative [[Kurt Bills]] by a margin of 35 percentage points (65.23% to 30.53%), carrying all but two counties.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.minnpost.com/national/2019/02/amy-klobuchar-may-be-minnesotas-most-popular-politician-but-how-popular-is-she-in-minnesotas-trump-country/|date=February 13, 2019|author=Greta Kaul|title=Amy Klobuchar may be Minnesota's most popular politician. But how popular is she in Minnesota's Trump country?|work=Minn Post|access-date=April 8, 2020|archive-date=October 17, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017085219/https://www.minnpost.com/national/2019/02/amy-klobuchar-may-be-minnesotas-most-popular-politician-but-how-popular-is-she-in-minnesotas-trump-country/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.sos.state.mn.us/elections-voting/2012-general-election-results/|publisher=Secretary of State of Minnesota|title=2012 General Election Results|access-date=April 8, 2020|archive-date=February 25, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200225073719/https://www.sos.state.mn.us/elections-voting/2012-general-election-results/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==== 2018 ====
{{main|2018 United States Senate election in Minnesota}}

Klobuchar ran for a third term and was reelected by a 24-point margin.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.minnpost.com/eric-black-ink/2019/01/why-amy-klobuchar-has-been-dubbed-the-queen-of-electability/|title=Why Amy Klobuchar has been dubbed 'the queen' of electability|last=Black|first=Eric|date=January 29, 2019|website=MinnPost|language=en-US|access-date=February 12, 2019|archive-date=October 16, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016173301/https://www.minnpost.com/eric-black-ink/2019/01/why-amy-klobuchar-has-been-dubbed-the-queen-of-electability/|url-status=live}}</ref> The Republican nominee was state representative [[Jim Newberger]]. The race was not seen as close, with Klobuchar outraising Newberger $9.9 million to $210,066 as of October 17. Klobuchar maintained a double-digit lead in the polls all autumn.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.startribune.com/amy-klobuchar-easily-wins-third-senate-term-tina-smith-defeats-gop-challenge-from-karin-housley/499710531/|title=Amy Klobuchar easily re-elected to Senate; Tina Smith turns back GOP challenge from Karin Housley|last=Keen|first=Judy|date=November 7, 2018|website=Star Tribune|access-date=February 12, 2019|archive-date=February 13, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190213064114/http://www.startribune.com/amy-klobuchar-easily-wins-third-senate-term-tina-smith-defeats-gop-challenge-from-karin-housley/499710531/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==== 2024 ====
{{main|2024 United States Senate election in Minnesota}}

In 2024, Klobuchar won a fourth term. In May, she won the DFL endorsement to be the Democratic nominee for the race.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Furguson |first1=Dana |title=U.S. Sen. Amy Klobuchar clinches DFL endorsement in reelection bid |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2024/05/31/us-sen-amy-klobuchar-clinches-dfl-endorsement-in-reelection-bid |website=MPR |date=May 31, 2024 |access-date=31 May 2024}}</ref> She won the primary election on August 13, and defeated Republican nominee [[Royce White]] in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2024 Minnesota Election Results |url=https://apnews.com/projects/election-results-2024/minnesota/ |access-date=2024-08-14 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref>

===Tenure===
A September 2009 poll found 58% of Minnesotans approved of the job Klobuchar was doing and 36% disapproved.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReport.aspx?g=e6a365a1-f86e-489d-804d-0828ab4d4eb3 | title=SurveyUSA News Poll #15748 | publisher=Surveyusa.com | access-date=July 20, 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090918062345/http://www.surveyusa.com/client/PollReport.aspx?g=e6a365a1-f86e-489d-804d-0828ab4d4eb3 | archive-date=September 18, 2009 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> On March 12, 2010, [[Rasmussen Reports]] indicated 67% of Minnesotans approved of the job she was doing. The ''[[Winona Daily News]]'' described her as a "rare politician who works across the aisle". [[Walter Mondale]] said, "She has done better in that miserable Senate than most people there."<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.winonadailynews.com/news/local/article_02c439dc-8114-11e1-9b0a-0019bb2963f4.html | title=Klobuchar a rare politician who works across the aisle | author=Brett Neely | website=Winona Daily News | date=April 8, 2012 | access-date=September 11, 2015 | archive-date=September 27, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180927112934/https://www.winonadailynews.com/news/local/article_02c439dc-8114-11e1-9b0a-0019bb2963f4.html | url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Ināra Mūrniece tiekas ar ASV Kongresa senatoriem (31790480172).jpg|thumb|Klobuchar with [[Lindsey Graham]] and [[John McCain]] in [[Latvia]] in 2016]]

At the end of the [[114th United States Congress|114th Congress]] in late 2016, Klobuchar had passed more legislation than any other senator.<ref name="Northwestern University">{{Cite news |url=http://medillonthehill.medill.northwestern.edu/2016/11/which-senators-have-passed-the-most-laws/ | title=Which Senators Have Passed the Most Laws? | author=Din, Benjamin | work=Northwestern University | access-date=November 28, 2017 | archive-date=October 20, 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191020170328/http://medillonthehill.medill.northwestern.edu/2016/11/which-senators-have-passed-the-most-laws/ | url-status=live }}</ref> In February 2017 she called for an independent, bipartisan commission to investigate ties between Russia and President [[Donald Trump]] and his administration. Concern about Trump's ties to Russia increased after reports that his campaign officials had repeated contact with senior Russian intelligence officials before the 2016 United States elections.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/14/us/politics/russia-intelligence-communications-trump.html | title=Trump Campaign Aides Had Repeated Contacts With Russian Intelligence | first1= Michael S. | last1=Schmidt | first2=Mark | last2=Mazzetti | first3=Matt | last3=Apuzzo| date=February 14, 2017 | newspaper=The New York Times | access-date=February 23, 2017 | issn=0362-4331 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170302025649/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/14/us/politics/russia-intelligence-communications-trump.html | archive-date=March 2, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.npr.org/2017/02/16/515529373/sen-amy-klobuchar-on-investigating-trump-and-russia | title=Sen. Amy Klobuchar On Investigating Trump And Russia | website=NPR.org | access-date=February 23, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170223192346/http://www.npr.org/2017/02/16/515529373/sen-amy-klobuchar-on-investigating-trump-and-russia | archive-date=February 23, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> Klobuchar had already signaled her interest in U.S.–Russia relations in December 2016 when she joined Republican senators [[John McCain]] and [[Lindsey Graham]] on a trip to the Baltic states and Ukraine.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.startribune.com/klobuchar-state-s-democrats-want-open-investigation-of-trump-russia-ties/414211633/ | title=Sen. Amy Klobuchar, state's Democrats want open investigation of Trump Russia ties |author=Sherry, Allison| newspaper=Star Tribune | access-date=February 23, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170222175806/http://www.startribune.com/klobuchar-state-s-democrats-want-open-investigation-of-trump-russia-ties/414211633/ | archive-date=February 22, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> She maintained high approval ratings throughout 2017, with an April 2017 ''[[Star Tribune]]'' poll placing her approval rating at 72%.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.startribune.com/minnesota-poll-results-senatorial-job-approval-ratings/420758934/ | title=Minnesota Poll results: What Minnesotans think of their senators |first1=Dennis|last1=McGrath|first2=Jeff|last2=Hargarten|first3=MaryJo|last3=Webster| newspaper=Star Tribune | access-date=November 28, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201034109/http://www.startribune.com/minnesota-poll-results-senatorial-job-approval-ratings/420758934/ | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> In October 2017 [[Morning Consult]] listed Klobuchar among the 10 senators with the highest approval ratings, and a November 2017 [[KSTP-TV]] poll put her approval rating at 56%.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://morningconsult.com/2017/10/31/americas-most-and-least-popular-senators-october-2017/ | title=America's Most and Least Popular Senators | work=Morning Consult | access-date=November 28, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171129013023/https://morningconsult.com/2017/10/31/americas-most-and-least-popular-senators-october-2017/ | archive-date=November 29, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2017/11/22/poll-minnesotans-sour-on-franken-after-sexual-misconduct-allegations | title=Franken's Minnesota support dims amid sexual misconduct claims | work=KSTP-TV | access-date=November 28, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201044852/https://www.mprnews.org/story/2017/11/22/poll-minnesotans-sour-on-franken-after-sexual-misconduct-allegations | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> An April 2019 Morning Consult poll found Klobuchar to be the third-most popular sitting senator, with a 58% approval rating and 26% disapproval rating, behind only Vermont senators [[Bernie Sanders]] and [[Patrick Leahy]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/440665-sanders-klobuchar-among-five-most-popular-senators-poll|title=Sanders, Klobuchar among five most popular senators: poll|first=Zack|last=Budryk|date=April 25, 2019|work=The Hill|access-date=July 8, 2019|archive-date=June 26, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230626155847/https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/440665-sanders-klobuchar-among-five-most-popular-senators-poll/|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Female senators.jpg|thumb|260px|Female senators of the 110th Congress, Klobuchar standing, second from the right, January 2007]]
According to the Center for Effective Lawmaking, Klobuchar scored "above expectations" with respect to how successful she was at moving significant legislation in the 115th Congress (2017–18).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thelawmakers.org/legislative-effectiveness-scores/highlights-from-the-new-115th-congress-legislative-effectiveness-scores|title=Highlights from the New 115th Congress Legislative Effectiveness Scores|last=Volden|first=Craig|date=February 27, 2019|work=Center for Effective Lawmaking|access-date=September 18, 2020|archive-date=June 8, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608053024/https://thelawmakers.org/legislative-effectiveness-scores/highlights-from-the-new-115th-congress-legislative-effectiveness-scores|url-status=live}}</ref>

During the [[Brett Kavanaugh Supreme Court nomination]] hearings in 2018, Kavanaugh gave heated responses to Klobuchar's questions about whether he had ever experienced [[Blackout (drug-related amnesia)|memory loss after consuming alcohol]], for which he later apologized.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2018/09/29/amy-klobuchars-big-brett-kavanaugh-moment-earned-rave-reviews-is-it-what-democrats-demand/?noredirect=on |title=Amy Klobuchar's big Brett Kavanaugh moment earned rave reviews. But is it what Democrats demand for 2020? |author1=Blake, Aaron |author2=Weigel, David |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=September 29, 2018 |access-date=February 11, 2019}}</ref>

There has been negative publicity about Klobuchar's treatment of her staff throughout her tenure in office.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 10, 2019 |title=Klobuchar's opening pitch sidetracked by staff horror stories |website=[[Politico]] |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2019/02/10/amy-klobuchar-2020-staff-horror-stories-1160780}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Flegenheimer |first1=Matt |last2=Ember |first2=Sydney |date=2019-02-22 |title=How Amy Klobuchar Treats Her Staff |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/22/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-staff.html |access-date=2024-07-08 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In February 2019, ''[[BuzzFeed News]]'' reported that interviews with former staffers and reviews of emails indicated that Klobuchar frequently abused and humiliated her employees, requiring significant staff time to manage her ire. The article reported that other employees found her to be "fair and effective" and a good boss.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/mollyhensleyclancy/amy-klobuchar-staff-2020-election |title=Staffers, Documents Show Amy Klobuchar's Wrath Toward Her Aides |author=Hensley-Clancy, Molly |work=BuzzFeed News |date=February 8, 2019 |access-date=February 19, 2019 |archive-date=February 18, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190218143147/https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/mollyhensleyclancy/amy-klobuchar-staff-2020-election |url-status=live }}</ref> ''[[Politico]]'' reported that Klobuchar had the highest annual staff turnover rate of any senator—36%—between 2011 and 2016.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2018/03/21/worst-bosses-congress-476729 |title=The 'Worst Bosses' in Congress? |author=McCaskill, Nolan D. |work=Politico |date=March 21, 2018 |access-date=February 19, 2019 |archive-date=February 18, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190218022210/https://www.politico.com/story/2018/03/21/worst-bosses-congress-476729 |url-status=live }}</ref> A ''[[Huffington Post]]'' article alleged she had a reputation for mistreating her staff, with some staff alleging she was prone to bursts of cruelty.<ref>{{cite web|title=Sen. Amy Klobuchar's Mistreatment Of Staff Scared Off Candidates To Manage Her Presidential Bid|first1=Molly|last1=Redden|first2=Amanda|last2=Terkel|website=HuffPost.com|date=February 6, 2019|url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/amy-klobuchar-abuse-staff-2020_n_5c5a1cb1e4b0871047588649|access-date=December 1, 2019|archive-date=October 23, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023234753/https://www.huffpost.com/entry/amy-klobuchar-abuse-staff-2020_n_5c5a1cb1e4b0871047588649|url-status=live}}</ref> In response to the negative reports, 61 former staffers wrote an open letter praising Klobuchar, stating that she was a caring "mentor and friend" to them.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thecut.com/2019/02/former-staffers-defend-amy-klobuchar-in-open-letter.html|title=Former Staffers Write Open Letter Defending Amy Klobuchar|last=Arnold|first=Amanda|date=February 25, 2019|website=The Cut|language=en-us|access-date=October 11, 2019|archive-date=October 17, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017180023/https://www.thecut.com/2019/02/former-staffers-defend-amy-klobuchar-in-open-letter.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the 115th Congress, she was absent for 0.5% of votes, with two-thirds of the senators missing more votes.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2018 Report Cards|work=[[GovTrack]]|url=https://www.govtrack.us/congress/members/report-cards/2018/senate/missed-votes|language=en|access-date=January 17, 2020|archive-date=October 17, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017180058/https://www.govtrack.us/congress/members/report-cards/2018/senate/missed-votes|url-status=live}}</ref> In the 116th Congress (2019–2021), during her presidential campaign, she had missed 39.1% of votes as of January 2020, making her the 5th-most absent senator.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://projects.propublica.org/represent/members/K000367/votes-missed/116|title=Missed Votes from Amy Klobuchar (D-Minn.)|last=Willis|first=Derek|website=ProPublica|date=August 12, 2015|language=en|access-date=January 2, 2020|archive-date=October 17, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017085238/https://projects.propublica.org/represent/members/K000367/votes-missed/116|url-status=live}}</ref>

Klobuchar was at the U.S. Capitol when Trump supporters [[2021 storming of the United States Capitol|stormed]] it on January 6, 2021. As ranking Democrat on the Senate Rules Committee, she and Senator [[Roy Blunt]] co-led Senate deliberations during the [[2021 United States Electoral College vote count]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Montemayor |first1=Stephen |title=Minnesota's Republican representatives silent as certification vote nears |url=https://www.startribune.com/minnesota-republicans-in-congress-silent-as-presidential-vote-approaches/600007045/ |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=Star Tribune |date=January 5, 2021 |archive-date=October 17, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017153636/https://www.startribune.com/minnesota-republicans-in-congress-silent-as-presidential-vote-approaches/600007045/ |url-status=live }}</ref> She also served as a teller, along with Blunt, Representative [[Rodney Davis (politician)|Rodney Davis]], and Representative [[Zoe Lofgren]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fox |first1=Lauren |last2=Mattingly |first2=Phil |title=The presidential electoral circus draws to an end in Congress |url=https://www.kten.com/story/43135305/the-presidential-electoral-circus-draws-to-an-end-in-congress |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=KTEN |agency=CNN |date=January 6, 2021 |language=en |archive-date=January 13, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210113061150/https://www.kten.com/story/43135305/the-presidential-electoral-circus-draws-to-an-end-in-congress |url-status=dead }}</ref> After Senate Republicans, led by Senator [[Ted Cruz]], objected to certifying Arizona's electoral votes, Klobuchar participated in the debate on the Senate floor. Shortly after she gave her remarks, the Capitol was breached.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mearhoff |first1=Sarah |title=Minn. delegation calls for peace after protesters storm US Capitol |url=https://www.pineandlakes.com/news/government-and-politics/6829664-As-US-Capitol-goes-on-lockdown-Minnesota-reps-tweet-shelter-in-DC |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=Pine and Lakes Echo Journal |date=January 6, 2021 |language=en |archive-date=February 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210202083402/https://www.pineandlakes.com/news/government-and-politics/6829664-As-US-Capitol-goes-on-lockdown-Minnesota-reps-tweet-shelter-in-DC |url-status=dead }}</ref> As the Senate adjourned, Klobuchar was alerted on her phone that shots were fired inside the Capitol, which she announced to those present.<ref name="Broadwater">{{cite news |last1=Broadwater |first1=Luke |last2=Cochrane |first2=Emily |title=Inside the Capitol, the Sound of the Mob Came First |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/01/06/us/politics/photos-capitol-building-protesters.html |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=January 7, 2021 |archive-date=October 18, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231018041455/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/01/06/us/politics/photos-capitol-building-protesters.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Immediately, Klobuchar, fellow senators, staff and journalists were evacuated from the chambers to a secure location.<ref name="Broadwater"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lesniewski |first1=Niels |last2=Tully-McManus |first2=Katherine |last3=McPherson |first3=Lindsey |title='Let's get back to work' — Mob doesn't deter Congress from electoral vote certification |url=https://www.rollcall.com/2021/01/06/lets-get-back-to-work-mob-doesnt-deter-congress-from-electoral-vote-certification/ |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=Roll Call |date=January 6, 2021 |language=en |archive-date=October 17, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231017085249/https://rollcall.com/2021/01/06/lets-get-back-to-work-mob-doesnt-deter-congress-from-electoral-vote-certification/ |url-status=live }}</ref> When the Capitol was secure, Congress reconvened and the election count was certified in the early morning of January 7. Klobuchar supported the certification. Later that day, Klobuchar said she supported the invocation of the [[Twenty-fifth Amendment to the United States Constitution]] to remove Trump from office "because you cannot have a president basically leading an insurrection against our own country's government."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mearhoff |first1=Sarah |title=Sen. Tina Smith, Minn. Democrats call for Trump to be removed from office |url=https://www.duluthnewstribune.com/news/government-and-politics/6831418-Sen.-Tina-Smith-Minn.-Democrats-call-for-Trump-to-be-removed-from-office |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=Duluth News Tribune |date=January 7, 2021 |language=en |archive-date=March 1, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210301103824/https://www.duluthnewstribune.com/news/government-and-politics/6831418-Sen.-Tina-Smith-Minn.-Democrats-call-for-Trump-to-be-removed-from-office |url-status=live }}</ref> She also called for investigations into the breach.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Salisbury |first1=Bill |title=Klobuchar to help lead probe of Capitol riot |url=https://www.twincities.com/2021/01/08/klobuchar-to-head-probe-of-capitol-riot/ |access-date=January 12, 2021 |work=Twin Cities |date=January 8, 2021 |archive-date=October 16, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016101442/https://www.twincities.com/2021/01/08/klobuchar-to-head-probe-of-capitol-riot/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

