==2020 presidential campaign==
{{Main|Amy Klobuchar 2020 presidential campaign}}

[[File:Amy Klobuchar with her husband John and daughter Abigail at her side, waves to the crowd after announcing her bid for the presidency at Boom Island Park in Minneapolis, Minnesota (46330801274).jpg|thumb|Klobuchar (center) with her husband and daughter at her campaign announcement]]
[[File:Amy Klobuchar 2020 presidential campaign logo.svg|thumb|Klobuchar campaign logo]]
''[[The New York Times]]'' and ''[[The New Yorker]]'' named Klobuchar as one of the women most likely to become the first female president of the United States,<ref>{{cite news | last=Zernike | first=Kate | date=May 18, 2008 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2008/05/18/weekinreview/18zernike.html | title=She Just Might Be President Someday | work=[[The New York Times]] | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170406175652/http://www.nytimes.com/2008/05/18/weekinreview/18zernike.html | archive-date=April 6, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Davidson Sorkin |first=Amy | date=December 12, 2016 | title=Thirteen Women Who Should Think About Running for President in 2020 |url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/amy-davidson/thirteen-women-who-should-think-about-running-for-president-in-2020 | magazine=[[The New Yorker]] | access-date=February 23, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170224190336/http://www.newyorker.com/news/amy-davidson/thirteen-women-who-should-think-about-running-for-president-in-2020 | archive-date=February 24, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> and [[MSNBC]] and ''The New Yorker'' named her as a possible nominee to the [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna30603525 | title=Practical female politico sought for court – Politics – Capitol Hill | last=Curry | first=Tom | work=[[NBC News]] | date=May 7, 2009 | access-date=July 20, 2010 | df=mdy-all | archive-date=July 21, 2013 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130721190904/http://www.nbcnews.com/id/30603525 | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/daily-comment/the-supreme-court-farm-team | title=The Supreme Court Farm Team | last=Toobin | first=Jeffrey | magazine=[[The New Yorker]] | date=March 17, 2014 | access-date=March 17, 2014 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021211724/http://www.newyorker.com/news/daily-comment/the-supreme-court-farm-team | archive-date=October 21, 2014 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

On February 10, 2019, Klobuchar announced her candidacy in the [[2020 Democratic Party presidential primaries]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Mitch |last2=Lerer |first2=Lisa |date=February 10, 2019 |title=Amy Klobuchar enters 2020 Presidential race |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/10/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-president-2020.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=February 10, 2019 |archive-date=February 27, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190227214656/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/10/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-president-2020.html |url-status=live }}</ref> She has said that she uses humor as one way to distinguish herself among the many other Democratic candidates in the 2020 campaign.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2019/07/17/742818947/minnesota-sen-amy-klobuchar-turns-to-humor-to-distinguish-herself-among-candidat|title=Minnesota Sen. Amy Klobuchar Turns To Humor To Distinguish Herself Among Candidates|author=Keith, Tamara|website=NPR.org|language=en|date=July 17, 2019|access-date=July 18, 2019|archive-date=September 12, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230912195916/https://www.npr.org/2019/07/17/742818947/minnesota-sen-amy-klobuchar-turns-to-humor-to-distinguish-herself-among-candidat|url-status=live}}</ref>

On January 19, 2020, ''The New York Times'' editorial board endorsed Klobuchar and [[Elizabeth Warren]] for president.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Democrats' Best Choices for President |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/01/19/opinion/amy-klobuchar-elizabeth-warren-nytimes-endorsement.html |access-date=January 20, 2020 |work=The New York Times |date=January 19, 2019 |archive-date=January 20, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200120040439/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/01/19/opinion/amy-klobuchar-elizabeth-warren-nytimes-endorsement.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=NY Times endorses Klobuchar, Warren in Democratic contest|work=Associated Press|date=January 19, 2020|url=https://apnews.com/c96d7fbf44fda339af5b4dd545eabed6|access-date=August 29, 2020|archive-date=November 28, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201128000816/https://apnews.com/c96d7fbf44fda339af5b4dd545eabed6|url-status=live}}</ref>

On March 2, 2020, the day before [[Super Tuesday]], Klobuchar suspended her campaign and endorsed [[Joe Biden]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Corasaniti |first1=Nick |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-drops-out.html |title=Amy Klobuchar Drops Out of Presidential Race and Plans to Endorse Biden |date=March 2, 2020 |work=The New York Times |access-date=March 2, 2020 |last2=Burns |first2=Alexander |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=March 2, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200302191523/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/02/us/politics/amy-klobuchar-drops-out.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

On May 21, 2020, it was reported that Biden asked several women, including Klobuchar, to undergo formal vetting for consideration as his vice-presidential running mate.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Biden asks Amy Klobuchar to undergo vetting to be running mate|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/biden-running-mate-klobuchar-vetting/|work=CBS News|first1=Bo|last1=Erickson|first2=Ed|last2=O'Keefe|language=en-US|access-date=May 21, 2020|archive-date=May 22, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200522172022/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/biden-running-mate-klobuchar-vetting/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Biden's vice president shortlist emerges, as Demings says she's being vetted|author=Edelman, Adam|date=May 22, 2020|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-s-vice-president-shortlist-emerges-demings-klobuchar-say-they-n1212481|website=NBC News|language=en|access-date=May 22, 2020|archive-date=October 3, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211003125728/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/biden-s-vice-president-shortlist-emerges-demings-klobuchar-say-they-n1212481|url-status=live}}</ref> On June 18, Klobuchar withdrew herself from consideration, saying that Biden should choose a woman of color.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/klobuchar-withdraws-veepstakes-says-biden-should-pick-woman-color-n1231501 |title=Klobuchar withdraws from veepstakes, says Biden should pick woman of color |work=NBC News |author=Ali Vitali |date=June 18, 2020 |access-date=June 18, 2020 |archive-date=May 14, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230514125624/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/klobuchar-withdraws-veepstakes-says-biden-should-pick-woman-color-n1231501 |url-status=live }}</ref>

