{{seealso|2026 Minnesota gubernatorial election}}
[[File:Amy Klobuchar 2026 DFL Convention.jpg|thumb|Klobuchar speaks at 2026 DFL Convention]]
On January 22, 2026, Klobuchar filed to run for [[governor of Minnesota]] after incumbent [[Tim Walz]] withdrew from the race.<ref name="gov1"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Klobuchar announces she's running for Minnesota governor after Walz dropped bid |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/klobuchar-announces-shes-running-for-minnesota-governor-after-walz-dropped-bid |access-date=September 4, 2026 |website=www.pbs.org}}</ref> On January 29, she officially confirmed her candidacy.<ref name="gov2"/> On May 29, she announced former [[Fergus Falls, Minnesota|Fergus Falls]] mayor Ben Schierer as her running mate.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cox |first=Peter |date=May 29, 2026 |title=Klobuchar picks a running mate as political party conventions get underway |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2026/05/29/former-fergus-falls-mayor-ben-schierer-picked-as-klobuchars-running-mate |website=MPR News |language=en-US |access-date=May 29, 2026}}</ref> On August 11, she became the first woman to win the [[Minnesota Democratic–Farmer–Labor Party|DFL]] nomination for governor.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Faircloth |first1=Ryan |last2=Ritter |first2=Sarah |date=August 11, 2026 |title=Klobuchar wins primary, becomes first woman to lead major ticket for Minnesota governor |url=https://kdhnews.com/news/politics/klobuchar-wins-primary-becomes-first-woman-to-lead-major-ticket-for-minnesota-governor/article_491d239e-4981-5ea8-a4da-626fc4ae7ec5.html |website=The Killeen Daily Herald |language=en-US |access-date=August 12, 2026}}</ref> She won the Democratic primary with 89.7% of the vote and will face Republican nominee [[Lisa Demuth]] in the general election.<ref>{{cite web |title=Minnesota Governor Primary Results 2026 |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/minnesota-governor-results |website=NBC News |language=en-US |date=August 11, 2026 |access-date=August 12, 2026}}</ref>

==Political positions==
{{main|Political positions of Amy Klobuchar}}

Klobuchar's political positions have generally been in line with [[modern liberalism in the United States|modern American liberalism]]. She is [[pro-choice]] on abortion, supports [[LGBT]] rights and the [[Affordable Care Act]], and was critical of the [[Iraq War]]. During the 115th Congress, she voted in line with President [[Donald Trump]]'s position on legislation 31.1 percent of the time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/congress-trump-score/|title=Tracking Congress In The Age Of Trump|last=Bycoffe|first=Aaron|date=January 30, 2017|website=[[FiveThirtyEight]]|location=New York City|language=en|access-date=January 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190102194848/https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/congress-trump-score/|archive-date=January 2, 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref>

According to [[GovTrack]], Klobuchar passed more legislation than any other senator by the end of the [[114th Congress]] in late 2016.<ref name="Northwestern University"/> According to [[Congress.gov]], {{as of|2018|December|16|lc=y|df=US}}, she had sponsored or co-sponsored 111 pieces of legislation that became law.<ref name=":1">{{cite web |url=https://www.congress.gov/member/amy-klobuchar/K000367?q=%7B%22bill-status%22:%22law%22%7D | title=Amy Klobuchar |last=Klobuchar|first=Amy |website=congress.gov |language=en | access-date=January 24, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910063240/https://www.congress.gov/member/amy-klobuchar/K000367?q=%7B%22bill-status%22%3A%22law%22%7D | archive-date=September 10, 2015 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref>

[[File:Senator Amy Klobuchar at Twin Cities Pride Parade 2018 - Minneapolis, Minnesota (42098802395).jpg|thumb|Klobuchar at Twin Cities [[Pride Parade]] in 2018]]
In 2023, the Lugar Center ranked Klobuchar in the top fifth of senators for bipartisanship.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Our Work |url=https://www.thelugarcenter.org/ourwork-85.html |access-date=2024-11-05 |website=www.thelugarcenter.org}}</ref>

==Personal life and family==
In 1993, Klobuchar married [[John Bessler]], a private practice attorney and a professor at the [[University of Baltimore School of Law]]. They have a daughter who graduated from Yale University and worked as a legislative director for New York councilman [[Keith Powers (politician)|Keith Powers]].<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Senator Amy Klobuchar's Daughter Abigail Bessler Is Following Her Mom's Political Footsteps |url=https://www.oprahmag.com/entertainment/a28510929/amy-klobuchar-daughter-abigail-bessler/ |work=The Oprah Magazine |author=Mitchell, Amanda |date=July 28, 2019 |access-date=November 11, 2019 |archive-date=December 4, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191204084318/https://www.oprahmag.com/entertainment/a28510929/amy-klobuchar-daughter-abigail-bessler/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Klobuchar is a member of the [[United Church of Christ]]<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.ucc.org/barack-obama-candidate|title=Barack Obama, candidate for President, is 'UCC'|work=United Church of Christ|access-date=October 17, 2018|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181130154125/http://www.ucc.org/barack-obama-candidate|archive-date=November 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> and is a cousin of musician [[Zola Jesus]].<ref>{{Cite magazine|last1=Ehrlich|first1=Brenna|date=February 5, 2020|title=Zola Jesus on Why She's Voting Bernie, Even Though She's Amy Klobuchar's Cousin|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/bernie-sanders-amy-klobuchar-cousin-zola-jesus-948261/|access-date=June 3, 2020|magazine=Rolling Stone|language=en-US|archive-date=December 4, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221204030608/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/bernie-sanders-amy-klobuchar-cousin-zola-jesus-948261/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Zola Jesus is Amy Klobuchar's cousin, still voting for Bernie Sanders|url=https://www.avclub.com/zola-jesus-is-amy-klobuchars-cousin-still-voting-for-b-1841450151|access-date=June 3, 2020|work=The A.V. Club|date=February 2, 2020|language=en-us|archive-date=March 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309061830/https://news.avclub.com/zola-jesus-is-amy-klobuchars-cousin-still-voting-for-b-1841450151|url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2021, Klobuchar revealed that she had been diagnosed with Stage 1A breast cancer in February 2021, that she had undergone a successful [[lumpectomy]], and that in May she had completed a course of [[Radiation therapy|radiation treatment]]. In August, her doctors determined that the treatments had all been successful and she was cancer-free.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Klobuchar|first=Amy|date=September 9, 2021|title=Statement from Senator Amy Klobuchar|url=https://medium.com/@Amy_Klobuchar/statement-from-senator-amy-klobuchar-975260e9de8|access-date=September 9, 2021|website=Medium|language=en|archive-date=September 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210909121901/https://medium.com/@Amy_Klobuchar/statement-from-senator-amy-klobuchar-975260e9de8|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2024, Klobuchar announced that she was still cancer-free after undergoing minor surgery and brief radiation treatment.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Weaver |first1=Al |title=Klobuchar cancer-free again after procedure |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/4768395-amy-klobuchar-cancer-free/ |work=The Hill |date=July 12, 2024 |access-date=12 July 2024}}</ref>

Klobuchar's grandparents were immigrants from [[Slovenia]]'s [[White Carniola]] region. Her paternal grandfather was a miner on Minnesota's [[Iron Range]].<ref name=svet>{{cite web|url=https://novice.svet24.si/clanek/novice/svet/5c0935b32ddfb/druga-slovenka-ki-bo-trumpu-spodnesla-tla|title=Druga Slovenka, ki bo Trumpu spodnesla tla|website=Svet24.si – Vsa resnica na enem mestu|access-date=February 10, 2019|archive-date=October 18, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231018214124/https://novice.svet24.si/clanek/novice/svet/5c0935b32ddfb/druga-slovenka-ki-bo-trumpu-spodnesla-tla|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=delo>{{cite web|url=https://www.delo.si/magazin/zanimivosti/po-sledeh-slovenskih-prednikov-amy-klobuchar-148291.html|title=Po sledeh slovenskih prednikov Amy Klobuchar|first1=Simona|first2=Simona|last1=Bandur|last2=Fajfar|date=February 11, 2019|website=delo.si|access-date=February 11, 2019|archive-date=February 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190211134004/https://www.delo.si/magazin/zanimivosti/po-sledeh-slovenskih-prednikov-amy-klobuchar-148291.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Her maternal grandparents emigrated from [[Switzerland]] to the United States.<ref>{{Cite news |first=Lori |last=Sturdevant |title=For Amy Klobuchar, if immigration then were like immigration now … |newspaper=[[Star Tribune]] |date=August 28, 2015 |url=http://www.startribune.com/for-klobuchar-if-immigration-then-were-like-immigration-now/323278261/ |quote=...includes a less familiar story about her mother's line, the Heubergers. Those grandparents were Swiss immigrants who settled in Milwaukee. |access-date=December 6, 2019 |archive-date=December 6, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191206163221/http://www.startribune.com/for-klobuchar-if-immigration-then-were-like-immigration-now/323278261/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Awards and honors==
Klobuchar has received a number of awards during her career. ''Minnesota Lawyer'' named her "Attorney of the Year" in 2001<ref name="Minnesota Lawyer" /> and [[Mothers Against Drunk Driving]] gave her a leadership award for advocating for successful passage of Minnesota's first felony DWI law.<ref name="Washington Post Live">{{Cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/postlive/amy-klobuchar/2013/04/11/50da5b5e-a2b2-11e2-9c03-6952ff305f35_story.html | title=Amy Klobuchar | date=April 11, 2013 | work=Washington Post Live| access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171206212002/https://www.washingtonpost.com/postlive/amy-klobuchar/2013/04/11/50da5b5e-a2b2-11e2-9c03-6952ff305f35_story.html | archive-date=December 6, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> ''[[Working Mother]]'' named her a 2008 "Best in Congress" for her efforts on behalf of working families, and ''[[The American Prospect]]'' named her a "woman to watch".<ref name="Washington Post Live"/>

In 2012, Klobuchar received the Sheldon Coleman Great Outdoors Award at a special Great Outdoors Week celebration presented by the American Recreation Coalition.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.americantrails.org/rtp/Senator-Klobuchar-Coleman-Award-2012.html | title=Senator Amy Klobuchar recognized for support of Recreational Trails Program | website=American Trails | access-date=September 18, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170918202015/http://www.americantrails.org/rtp/Senator-Klobuchar-Coleman-Award-2012.html | archive-date=September 18, 2017 | url-status=dead | df=mdy-all }}</ref> She was one of the recipients of the Agricultural Retailers Association's 2012 Legislator of the Year Award, alongside Republican representative [[John Mica]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.aradc.org/HigherLogic/System/DownloadDocumentFile.ashx?DocumentFileKey=af67ac9b-8f1a-42c3-a65f-db25e758c0e4 | title=Senator Amy Klobuchar (D-MN) and Congressman John Mica (R-FL) Named ARA Legislators of the Year | last=Hummell | first=Michelle | date=March 11, 2013 | work=Agricultural Retailers Association | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-date=September 30, 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150930233849/http://www.aradc.org/HigherLogic/System/DownloadDocumentFile.ashx?DocumentFileKey=af67ac9b-8f1a-42c3-a65f-db25e758c0e4 | url-status=dead }}</ref> In 2013, Klobuchar received an award for her leadership in the fight to prevent sexual assault in the military at a national summit hosted by the Service Women's Action Network (SWAN).<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.klobuchar.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/news-releases?ID=F6D5081B-88BD-4B85-B1F3-ECAE232CF449 | title=Klobuchar Receives Award for Work Fighting Sexual Assault in the Military | date=April 18, 2013 | work=US Senator Amy Klobuchar | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201080840/https://www.klobuchar.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/news-releases?ID=F6D5081B-88BD-4B85-B1F3-ECAE232CF449 | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> Also, in 2013, she received a Friend of CACFP award for her leadership in passing the Healthy Hunger Free Kids act and her efforts to set new nutrition standards for all meals served in the CACFP by the National [[Child and Adult Care Food Program]] (CACFP) Sponsors Association.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.cacfp.org/about-us/friend-cacfp-award-winners/2013-senator-amy-klobuchar/ | title=2013 Friend of CACFP – Senator Amy Klobuchar, Minnesota | date=2013 | work=National CACFP Sponsors Association | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201033434/https://www.cacfp.org/about-us/friend-cacfp-award-winners/2013-senator-amy-klobuchar/ | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> Klobuchar and [[Senator Al Franken]] received the 2014 Friends of Farm Bureau Award from the Minnesota branch of the [[American Farm Bureau Federation]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.fbmn.org/blog/2014/10/31/senators-klobuchar-and-franken-receive-minnesota-farm-bureau-friend-of-farm-bureau-award | title=Senators Klobuchar and Franken Receive Minnesota Farm Bureau "Friend of Farm Bureau" Award | date=October 31, 2014 | work=Minnesota Farm Bureau | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201040055/http://www.fbmn.org/blog/2014/10/31/senators-klobuchar-and-franken-receive-minnesota-farm-bureau-friend-of-farm-bureau-award | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> She received the [[American Bar Association]]'s Congressional Justice Award in 2015 for her efforts to protect vulnerable populations from violence, exploitation, and assault and to eliminate discrimination in the workplace.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.americanbar.org/news/abanews/aba-news-archives/2015/04/aba_honors_minnesota.html | title=ABA honors Minnesota Sen. Amy Klobuchar for efforts to protect vulnerable populations, eliminate workplace discrimination | date=April 13, 2015 | work=American Bar Association | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201042539/https://www.americanbar.org/news/abanews/aba-news-archives/2015/04/aba_honors_minnesota.html | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> Also in 2015 the [[National Consumers League]] honored Klobuchar with the Trumpeter Award for her work "on regulation to strengthen consumer product safety legislation, on ensuring a fair and competitive marketplace, and increasing accessibility to communications, specifically in the wireless space".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.klobuchar.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/amy-in-the-news?ID=033BC9FF-451E-4E7F-8B4F-AE053F34ADEC | title=Sen. Klobuchar gets top honor from National Consumers League | date=October 7, 2015 | work=MinnPost | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201033150/https://www.klobuchar.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/amy-in-the-news?ID=033BC9FF-451E-4E7F-8B4F-AE053F34ADEC | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> In 2016, she received the Goodwill Policymaker Award from [[Goodwill Industries]] for her commitment to the nonprofit sector and leading the Nonprofit Energy Efficiency Act.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.goodwilleasterseals.org/about/blog/amy-klobuchar-honored | title=Senator Amy Klobuchar Honored with Goodwill's Policymaker Award| date=2016 | work=Goodwill | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201080925/https://www.goodwilleasterseals.org/about/blog/amy-klobuchar-honored | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> In 2017, she received the Arabella Babb Mansfield Award from the [[National Association of Women Lawyers]]<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.mwlawyers.org/news/344715/MWL-Congratulates-2017-NAWL-Annual-Awardees-Senator-Amy-Klobuchar-and-Hon.-Wilhelmina-M.-Wright.htm | title=MWL Congratulates 2017 NAWL Annual Awardees: Senator Amy Klobuchar and Hon. Wilhelmina M. Wright | last=Zuercher | first=Hannah | date=May 10, 2017 | work=Minnesota Women Lawyers | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201041346/http://www.mwlawyers.org/news/344715/MWL-Congratulates-2017-NAWL-Annual-Awardees-Senator-Amy-Klobuchar-and-Hon.-Wilhelmina-M.-Wright.htm | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> and was chosen as the Mary Louise Smith Chair in Women and Politics for the Carrie Chapman Catt Center at [[Iowa State University]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.thinkames.com/event/en-amy-klobuchar-mary-louise-smith-chair-women-politics/ | title=Sen. Amy Klobuchar as the Mary Louise Smith Chair in Women and Politics | date=August 31, 2017 | work=Think Ames | access-date=November 28, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201035852/https://www.thinkames.com/event/en-amy-klobuchar-mary-louise-smith-chair-women-politics/ | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> In 2021, Klobuchar received the Award for Distinguished Public Service from the [[Association of American Publishers]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://publishers.org/news/klobuchar-receives-aap-2021-award/|title=Senator Amy Klobuchar To Receive AAP'S 2021 Award for Distinguished Public Service|date=May 6, 2021|access-date=May 7, 2021|archive-date=May 7, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507210029/https://publishers.org/news/klobuchar-receives-aap-2021-award/|url-status=live}}</ref> During an official visit to Ukraine in August 2022, she was awarded the Ukrainian Order of Merit, First Class from [[President Zelenskyy]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-08-31 |title=Sen. Klobuchar meets with President Zelenskyy during trip to Ukraine |url=https://www.klobuchar.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/2022/8/sen-klobuchar-meets-with-president-zelenskyy-during-trip-to-ukraine|access-date=2026-01-31 |language=en}}</ref> In 2024, she received the Distinguished Public Service Award from The American Legion.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-03-01 |title=Klobuchar receives American Legion's Distinguished Public Service award |url=https://www.dglobe.com/news/local/klobuchar-receives-american-legions-distinguished-public-service-award |access-date=2024-10-22 |website=Worthington Globe |language=en}}</ref>

==Publications==

=== Books ===
{{external media| float = right| video1 = [https://www.c-span.org/video/?327493-1/words-senator-amy-klobuchar ''After Words'' interview with Amy Klobuchar on ''The Senator Next Door'', September 12, 2015], [[C-SPAN]]}}
Klobuchar has written four books. In 1986, she published ''Uncovering the Dome'', a case study of the 10-year political struggle to build the [[Hubert H. Humphrey Metrodome]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.minnpost.com/books/2015/08/klobuchars-senator-next-door-centers-people-who-shaped-her-politics | title=Klobuchar's 'The Senator Next Door' centers on the people who shaped her politics | last=Goetzman | first=Amy | date=August 28, 2015 | work=[[MinnPost]] | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201033334/https://www.minnpost.com/books/2015/08/klobuchars-senator-next-door-centers-people-who-shaped-her-politics | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> In 2015, she published an autobiography, ''The Senator Next Door: A Memoir from the Heartland''.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/amy-klobuchar/the-senator-next-door/ | title=The Senator Next Door | date=July 1, 2015 | work=[[Kirkus Reviews]] | access-date=November 21, 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201080759/https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/amy-klobuchar/the-senator-next-door/ | archive-date=December 1, 2017 | url-status=live | df=mdy-all }}</ref> In 2021, ''Antitrust: Taking on Monopoly Power from the Gilded Age to the Digital Age'' was published, a sprawling, 624-page historical overview of antitrust law in the United States, up to the current regulatory issues facing [[Big Tech]], the American public, and the world.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/amy-klobuchar/antitrust/ | title=Antitrust: Taking On Monopoly Power from the Gilded Age to the Digital Age | date=March 2, 2021 | work=[[Kirkus Reviews]] | access-date=April 19, 2021 | df=mdy-all | archive-date=April 19, 2021 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210419215127/https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/amy-klobuchar/antitrust/ | url-status=live }}</ref> In 2023, she published ''The Joy of Politics''.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/amy-klobuchar/the-joy-of-politics/ |title=The Joy of Politics |website=Kirkus Reviews |language=en |access-date=September 6, 2023 |archive-date=September 6, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230906233137/https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/amy-klobuchar/the-joy-of-politics/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

